# Where Wyrd fits

Wyrd sits between the people and agents who build AI components and the systems
that run, version, and govern them. It is not a training framework, not an
application runtime, and not a cloud platform. It is the typed layer that records
what those systems declare and observes what they do.

## The narrow waist

An AI stack has many tools above and below any given layer: data platforms,
training frameworks, serving infra, agent frameworks, eval harnesses. They do not
share a vocabulary. Wyrd is the **narrow waist** — one typed envelope (the Card)
that every layer can produce and every layer can read.

A dataset produced by a feature pipeline, a model from a training job, and a
prompt from a repo all become the same shape once registered. The tools above and
below Wyrd stay diverse; the contract between them is uniform. That is what makes
lineage, policy, and observation possible across tools that were never designed
to interoperate.

## Mitari, Wyrd, and Fathom

**Mitari** is the platform. It has two layers:

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Layer', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'What it is' },
    { header: 'Model' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['Wyrd', 'The open-core substrate — the typed envelope, registry, lineage, and Skald runtime. The narrow waist of the AI stack.', 'Open core'],
    ['Fathom', 'The enterprise intelligence layer. It reads Wyrd’s typed model and turns runtime behavior into evidence — leakage, eval, drift, and audit.', 'Enterprise']
  ]}
/>

The division is deliberate: **Wyrd captures every stage; Fathom reasons across all
of it.** Wyrd's job is to make behavior recordable and typed. Fathom's job is to
verify that behavior against what each component declared — to turn opinion into
evidence that no single point tool can see.

<Aside title="Fathom is not shipped yet" data-variant="not">

Fathom is the enterprise layer of the Mitari platform and is not yet available.
This documentation covers Wyrd, the open-core substrate. Fathom is described here
only to explain where Wyrd fits.

</Aside>

## The lifecycle loop

Wyrd is built for a loop, not a one-shot pipeline:

```text
Develop → Deploy → Observe → Diagnose → (fix back to code) → Develop
```

- **Develop** — build and connect typed components on Wyrd. Traces and
  experiments stream in.
- **Deploy** — models, services, and agents ship as declared cards with a
  versioned spec.
- **Observe** — runtime behavior is recorded as runs and observations, measured
  against what each card declared.
- **Diagnose** — the intelligence layer isolates a failure, proves the cause, and
  returns a fix as code.

Keep this loop distinct from two other axes that use similar words:

- The **per-card journey** — Author → Register → Refer → Run — is how one card
  moves through the system.
- The card **`status.phase`** — local → registered → active → deprecated — is one
  card's lifecycle state.

They are three different things. Do not merge them.

## What Wyrd is not

Wyrd records, validates, governs, links, installs, observes, and exposes AI
components. It does not run them.

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Wyrd owns', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Your code owns' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['The registry, lineage graph, and card contracts', 'Inference loops and training loops'],
    ['Policy evaluation and audit records', 'App servers and agent processes'],
    ['Runs, observations, and drift signals', 'Deployment mechanics'],
    ['The typed surfaces (HTTP, SDK, CLI, MCP)', 'The business logic inside a run']
  ]}
/>

Runtime results that persist become runs, observations, artifact cards,
relationships, audit records, or status updates — through service-owned paths, not
by writing runtime objects into specs. Your process stays yours; Wyrd is the
observation and governance layer around it.

